+/* Is UDP already holding more than the cache can usefully keep?
+ *
+ * What costs throughput is not one socket's buffer but the total the receive
+ * path is touching: the NIC's descriptor pages plus every socket's queued
+ * data.  Measured on a single core, goodput is flat while that total stays
+ * inside the last level cache and falls off sharply past it - and the cliff
+ * moves with the cache, not with the buffer: eight sockets at 2MB collapse at
+ * the same aggregate as one socket at 16MB, and masking L3 ways with CAT drags
+ * the knee down in proportion.
+ *
+ * So the bound has to be global.  sk_memory_allocated() already aggregates
+ * across every UDP socket, which is exactly the quantity that matters, so no
+ * new accounting is needed.
+ *
+ * The percentage is left to the operator because the kernel cannot see how
+ * much of the cache is already spoken for: the NIC's rings are invisible from
+ * here (on mlx5 at a 9000-byte MTU a 1024-entry ring pins 16MB), and on a busy
+ * machine other cores are competing for the same cache.  Lower it when either
+ * is true.
+ */
+/* Bytes of receive buffer autotuning has handed out across every UDP socket.
+ *
+ * The first attempt bounded sk_memory_allocated() instead, and it does not
+ * work: that counts memory already queued, and while the consumer keeps up the
+ * queues sit nearly empty - 0.75MB held against 8MB of granted capacity in one
+ * measurement.  So the budget never bites until the queues have already grown
+ * deep, by which point the buffers behind them are several doublings too large.
+ * Occupancy follows capacity rather than the other way round, so capacity is
+ * what has to be bounded.
+ */
+static atomic_long_t udp_rcvbuf_granted;
+
+/* One step of additive increase.  Small enough that a socket overshooting its
