+	 * re-charge if it changes.
+	 *
+	 * Note what this does and does not do: the request is still honoured in
+	 * full, because refusing a size the application asked for outright would
+	 * break a long-standing expectation.  What it buys is that everyone else
+	 * stops compounding the problem - which is the whole difficulty, since a
+	 * 64MB buffer is entirely safe until it is the eighth one.
+	 */
+	/* Charge the buffer the socket already has, once.
+	 *
+	 * The budget bounds what UDP is holding, and the buffer a socket
+	 * starts with is part of that no less than what autotuning adds
+	 * later.  Counting only the additions leaves the baseline invisible,
+	 * and the baseline is not small when there are many sockets: eight at
+	 * a 1MB default are 8MB spoken for before a single grant is made,
+	 * which is most of a budget set at half of an 18MB cache.
+	 *
+	 * Measured with the additions alone counted: eight sockets reached
